# Refactor Phase Rules

> ⚠ Temporary. Delete `refactor-phase.mdc` after full refactor is shipped.

## R1 — Simplify. One direction. No duplicate paths.

- Every system must have **one entry point, one write path, one event chain**.
- Re-subscribe, duplicate listeners, parallel stacks → delete, not comment out.
- Unidirectional flow only: trigger → handler → state change → UI. No circular wiring.
- If two classes do the same thing, pick one and delete the other.

## R2 — Refactor together. No isolated patches.

- When a system is touched, fix **all** related issues in that system at the same time.
- Do not patch one symptom while leaving the root cause. Fix root, then remove symptoms.
- Large-scale edits: touch all affected files in one pass. Do not split related fixes across separate PRs/sessions unless the user explicitly asks.

## R3 — Network-first. Delete offline code. (See also architecture.mdc)

- There is no offline mode. Solo = `OnlineHost` NGO. Do not add or preserve offline branches.
- Any variable, flag, condition, or comment referencing "offline", "!isOnline", "solo offline", "isOnline == false" → **delete**.
- Cheer system exception: solo self-cheer is allowed (`ActivePlayerCount == 1`) — this is an **online** solo rule, not offline.

## R4 — Reference standard NGO patterns. Your game is not special.

- When designing a system, first ask: "how does a standard NGO co-op game handle this?"
- If the answer is simpler than the current implementation, the current implementation is wrong.
- Do not invent custom solutions for problems that NGO already solves (spawn, scene sync, despawn).
- Player lifecycle: other games use **scene-scoped spawn + despawn**. Match that model.

## R5 — Full release scope. No demo-only thinking.

- Every fix and feature targets the **full shipped game**, not just the demo slice.
- Do not add "demo-only" conditionals or stub out systems for later.
- Demo and release share code — build it right once.

